{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>These are the waters of Merivah, etc.</b> One needs to know why He repeated saying <b>Merivah, which they strove</b>, and it would not have sufficed to say ״These are the waters concerning which the children of strove,״ etc. Furthermore, His saying <b>they strove against HaShem</b> indicates that He is speaking about Israel, while His saying <b>and He was sanctified through them</b> implies that He is speaking about Moses and Aaron." "And it appears that the verse comes to give a reason for His, blessed be He, being stringent concerning the sanctity of His name in this matter, and He said <b>These are the waters of Merivah</b>, etc. And this merivah the verse justifies by saying <b>which they strove</b> — meaning, they lodged a complaint against HaShem, and they were justified in the claim of their contention. <b>And He was sanctified through them</b> — meaning, HaShem did things to become sanctified among them, that is, among Israel, in that He said to Moses to speak to the rock that the children of Israel would choose, for through this they would see the power of the Sovereign: that even though He brought them into the desert, not a place, etc., and there was no water, He would bring forth water for them by His utterance from any kind of rock that might be before them. But Moses and Aaron did not do so; therefore HaShem was insistent with them and said ״Therefore,״ etc. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 19:5): <b>and He was sanctified through them</b> — in Moses and Aaron, in that He decreed upon them that they would not bring them in, etc. And there are seventy faces to the Torah."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:17.621952906Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}